<section id="theory" class="tab-content active">

<h2 class="section-title">
Complete Theory
</h2>


<div class="card definition">

<h2>1. What is Force?</h2>

<p>
Force is a push or pull acting on an object which can change
the state of rest, state of motion, direction of motion,
or shape of the object.
</p>

<div class="formula">
<span class="formula-title">SI Unit</span>
Newton (N)
</div>

<div class="success">
<strong>1 Newton:</strong> The force required to produce an
acceleration of 1 m/s² in a mass of 1 kg.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>2. Effects of Force</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">
<h3>Change in State</h3>
<p>
A force can make a stationary object move or stop a moving object.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Change in Speed</h3>
<p>
Force can increase or decrease the speed of an object.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Change in Direction</h3>
<p>
Force can change the direction of motion.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Change in Shape</h3>
<p>
Force can deform an object.
</p>
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>3. Force is a Vector Quantity</h2>

<p>
Force has both magnitude and direction, therefore force is a
<strong>vector quantity</strong>.
</p>

<div class="info">
Examples of other vector quantities include velocity,
acceleration, displacement and momentum.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>4. Types of Force</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">
<h3>Contact Force</h3>
<p>
Force which acts only when objects are in physical contact.
</p>

<ul>
<li>Friction</li>
<li>Normal Reaction</li>
<li>Muscular Force</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Non-Contact Force</h3>
<p>
Force which acts without physical contact.
</p>

<ul>
<li>Gravitational Force</li>
<li>Magnetic Force</li>
<li>Electrostatic Force</li>
</ul>
</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>5. Balanced and Unbalanced Forces</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">

<h3>Balanced Force</h3>

<p>
When the resultant force acting on an object is zero,
the forces are called balanced forces.
</p>

<div class="formula">
F<sub>net</sub> = 0
</div>

<p>
Balanced forces do not produce acceleration.
</p>

</div>


<div class="mini">

<h3>Unbalanced Force</h3>

<p>
When the resultant force is not zero, the forces are
called unbalanced forces.
</p>

<div class="formula">
F<sub>net</sub> ≠ 0
</div>

<p>
Unbalanced force produces acceleration.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>6. Resultant Force</h2>

<p>
The single force which produces the same effect as all
the forces acting together is called the resultant force.
</p>

<div class="formula">
F<sub>resultant</sub> = Vector Sum of all forces
</div>

<div class="warning">
For forces acting in the same direction, add them.
For opposite directions, subtract them.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>7. Mass and Force</h2>

<p>
Mass is the quantity of matter in an object, whereas force
is an interaction capable of changing its motion.
</p>

<div class="table-wrap">

<table>

<tr>
<th>Mass</th>
<th>Force</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Scalar quantity</td>
<td>Vector quantity</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>SI unit: kilogram</td>
<td>SI unit: Newton</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Does not depend on location</td>
<td>Can depend on acceleration and interaction</td>
</tr>

</table>

</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>8. Inertia</h2>

<p>
Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist any change
in its state of rest or motion.
</p>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">
<h3>Inertia of Rest</h3>
<p>
An object at rest tends to remain at rest.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Inertia of Motion</h3>
<p>
A moving object tends to continue moving.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Inertia of Direction</h3>
<p>
An object resists change in its direction of motion.
</p>
</div>

</div>

<div class="success">
Mass is a measure of inertia. Greater mass means greater inertia.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>9. Momentum</h2>

<p>
Momentum is the product of mass and velocity.
</p>

<div class="formula">
p = mv
</div>

<div class="formula">
SI Unit = kg·m/s
</div>

<p>
Momentum is a vector quantity because velocity is a vector.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>10. Impulse</h2>

<p>
Impulse is the product of force and the time for which the
force acts.
</p>

<div class="formula">
J = FΔt
</div>

<p>
Impulse is equal to the change in momentum.
</p>

<div class="formula">
J = Δp
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>11. Friction</h2>

<p>
Friction is a contact force which opposes relative motion
or the tendency of relative motion between surfaces.
</p>

<div class="grid">

<div class="mini">
<h3>Static Friction</h3>
<p>
Acts when there is no relative motion between surfaces.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Sliding Friction</h3>
<p>
Acts when one surface slides over another.
</p>
</div>

<div class="mini">
<h3>Rolling Friction</h3>
<p>
Acts when an object rolls over a surface.
</p>
</div>

</div>

<div class="success">
Rolling friction is generally less than sliding friction.
</div>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>12. Normal Reaction</h2>

<p>
Normal reaction is the force exerted by a surface on an
object in contact with it, acting perpendicular to the surface.
</p>

<div class="formula">
N = mg
</div>

<p>
For a stationary object on a horizontal surface, in the
simple case where no other vertical forces act.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>13. Gravitational Force</h2>

<p>
Every object in the universe attracts every other object
because of gravity.
</p>

<div class="formula">
F = Gm₁m₂/r²
</div>

<p>
This is Newton's law of universal gravitation.
</p>

</div>


<div class="card">

<h2>14. Weight</h2>

<p>
Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object.
</p>

<div class="formula">
W = mg
</div>

<div class="warning">
Mass remains essentially constant from place to place,
while weight changes when the gravitational acceleration changes.
</div>

</div>


</section>


